<exercise>
	<title>Angle for zero dot product</title>

	<statement><p>
		In <xref ref="exercisegroup-vector-geom-guess-orthog" text="type-local">Exercises</xref>,
		you determined pairs of nonzero vectors <m> \uvec{u}, \uvec{v} </m> so that <m> \udotprod{u}{v} = 0 </m>.
		In all such cases,
		is the angle between those vectors?
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		We know that the angle <m> \theta </m> between nonzero vectors <m> \uvec{u}, \uvec{v} </m> satisfies
		<md> \cos\theta = \frac{\udotprod{u}{v}}{ \unorm{u} \unorm{v} } </md>.
		If the dot product is <m> 0 </m>,
		then the whole right-hand side of the above equation is <m> 0 </m>.
		The only angle in the domain <m> 0 \le \theta \le \pi </m> for which <m> \cos\theta = 0 </m> is <m> \theta = \pi / 2 </m>,
		meaning that in each case in <xref ref="exercisegroup-vector-geom-guess-orthog" text="type-local">Exercises</xref>,
		the vectors form a right angle.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

