<exercise>
	<title>Comparing angles</title>

	<statement><p>
		Demonstrate that the angle between
		<m> \uvec{u} = (1,2,-3) </m> and <m> \uvec{w} = (19,3,-15) </m>
		is the same as the angle between
		<m> \uvec{v} = (59,-22,-9) </m> and <m> \uvec{w} </m>,
		without computing the angles themselves.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Instead of computing the angles,
		we compute the cosines of the angles.
		<md>
			<mrow>
				\cos\theta_{\uvec{u},\uvec{w}} \amp = \frac{\udotprod{u}{w}}{\unorm{u} \unorm{w}}
				\amp
				\cos\theta_{\uvec{v},\uvec{w}} \amp = \frac{\udotprod{v}{w}}{\unorm{v} \unorm{w}}
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp = \frac{70}{\sqrt{14} \sqrt{595}}
				\amp
				\amp = \frac{1190}{\sqrt{4046} \sqrt{595}}
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp \amp
				\amp = \frac{1190}{17 \sqrt{14} \sqrt{595}}
			</mrow><mrow>
				\amp \amp
				\amp = \frac{70}{\sqrt{14} \sqrt{595}}
			</mrow>
		</md>
		Since <m> \cos\theta_{\uvec{u},\uvec{w}} = \cos\theta_{\uvec{v},\uvec{w}} </m> and the cosine function is <term>one-to-one</term> on the domain <m> 0 \le \theta \le \pi </m>,
		we conclude <m> \theta_{\uvec{u},\uvec{w}} = \theta_{\uvec{v},\uvec{w}} </m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Norm of a difference</title>

	<task><p>
		Use a diagram to geometrically illustrate that
		<xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-norm-algebra-diff">Rule</xref>
		of <xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-norm-algebra" />
		is true.
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		Use the formulas for vector subtraction
		(<xref ref="subsubsection-vectors-concepts-vec-subtract" />)
		and <xref ref="definition-vector-geom-norm" text="custom">vector norm</xref>
		to verify
		<xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-norm-algebra-diff">Rule</xref>
		of <xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-norm-algebra" />.
		Be sure to use proper left-hand-side versus right-hand-side procedure!
	</p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise>
	<title>Algebra of the dot product</title>

	<statement><p>
	Using the matrix-multiplication formulation of the dot product
	(<xref ref="subsection-vector-geom-concepts-dot-prod-vs-matrix-mult" />),
	for each of the rules in <xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-dot-prod-algebra" />
	(excluding <xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-dot-prod-algebra-vs-norm">Rule</xref>)
	find a rule or combination of rules in <xref ref="proposition-matrix-ops-algebra-rules" /> to justify it.
	</p></statement>

	<hint><p>
		For <xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-dot-prod-algebra-commute" />
		of <xref ref="proposition-vector-geom-dot-prod-algebra" />,
		consider that every <m> 1 \times 1 </m> matrix is <xref ref="definition-special-forms-symmetric" text="title" />,
		and that under the matrix-multiplication formulation of the dot product,
		the result of a dot product is technically a <m> 1 \times 1 </m> matrix.
	</p></hint>

</exercise>

<!-- Exercise group -->
<xi:include href="./exercises.d/dot-prod-vs-smul-algebra.ptx" />

<exercise>
	<title>Norm of a sum</title>

	<task><p>
		Provide an example of two-dimensional vectors <m> \uvec{u}, \uvec{v} </m> where all of the components of the two vectors are positive numbers,
		and where <m> \norm{ \uvec{u} + \uvec{v} } </m> is <em> strictly less than </em> <m> \unorm{u} + \unorm{v} </m>.
		Draw a diagram of your vectors in the <m> xy </m>-plane that geometrically illustrates <em> why </em> this relationship of norms is true in your example.
	</p></task>

	<task><p>
		Provide an example of two-dimensional vectors <m> \uvec{u}, \uvec{v} </m> where <m> \norm{ \uvec{u} + \uvec{v} } </m> is <em> exactly equal to </em> <m> \unorm{u} + \unorm{v} </m>.
		What geometric relationship must there be between <m> \uvec{u} </m> and <m> \uvec{v} </m> for this to work out?
	</p></task>

</exercise>

</exercises>
